<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganesha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ganapati, Vinayaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ganesha is an elephant-headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Parvati, worshipped as the remover of obstacles and the patron of beginnings, learning and the arts. He is invoked at the start of rituals, journeys and undertakings, and is honoured across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and in Buddhist and Jain traditions. His annual festival, Ganesh Chaturthi, is especially popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganesha.jpg" alt="Ganesha" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganesha_Basohli_miniature_circa_1730_Dubost_p73.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim Adil Shah II, who praised the goddess Saraswati and the god <b>Ganesha</b> in his songs, was remembered for his openness towards Hindu subjects, musicians and scholars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 3rd century, as Buddhism spread under the Northern Wei, strong influences from the Indian subcontinent entered the native religion: a cult of <b>Ganesha</b> is attested in 531, and processions of carts and floats bearing gods’ images, with music and chanting, came the same way.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ganesha</b>, the elephant-headed remover of obstacles, is honoured at the start of almost every undertaking.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first section says that <b>Ganesha</b> wrote the text down to Vyasa's dictation, but scholars regard this as a later interpolation, and the Critical Edition does not include it.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Joyce Flueckinger and Paul Brians call it a &quot;universal aarti&quot;, and Om Lata Bahadur notes its use for gods as different as Krishna, <b>Ganesha</b> and Murugan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/om-jai-jagdish-hare">Om Jai Jagdish Hare</a></li></ul></section>
